From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 03:10:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xfrm: drop the inner priority when the outer DSCP is not copied

ip_forward() sets skb->priority from the TOS of a forwarded IPv4
packet, unless net.ipv4.ip_forward_update_priority is cleared, and a
tunnel mode SA leaves that priority on the packet it builds. A priority
can also arrive with the inner packet, for example from the ingress QoS
map of a VLAN device. Qdiscs and drivers that schedule by priority then
put the inner classes of one SA into different flows or queues.
fq_codel uses the minor number of a priority whose major number equals
its handle as the flow index instead of the flow hash. The default
qdiscs that mq attaches to the tx queues have handle 0, so every
non-zero priority from rt_tos2priority() selects a fixed flow of its
own. mqprio and some multiqueue drivers map the priority to a tx queue.
The SA numbers its packets before they are queued, and the peer drops
a packet that falls behind its replay window.

On EN7581 with a software ESP SA that does not copy the DSCP, all ESP
packets in one tx queue and one flow hash for the SA, a ping flood with
DSCP AF21, which rt_tos2priority() maps to priority 2, next to an
upload through the tunnel raised XfrmInStateSeqError of the peer by
255 in 10 s. With a POSTROUTING rule that set the priority of the ESP
packets to 0, it rose by 1.

A tunnel mode SA with XFRM_SA_XFLAG_DONT_ENCAP_DSCP does not copy the
inner DSCP to the outer header, so the outer packet carries no class of
the inner packet on the wire. Drop the priority of the inner packet as
well, for software states once the outer header has been added, and for
packet offload states before the packet is handed to the driver or sent
to the device. This also keeps the inner class out of the PCP that a
VLAN egress QoS map derives from the priority. Each tunnel state of a
nested bundle that has the flag drops it.

Only priorities with major number 0 are dropped: the values from
rt_tos2priority(), from an unprivileged SO_PRIORITY and from a VLAN
ingress QoS map, and any other such value set on the inner packet. A
priority with a major number is a qdisc class id, set explicitly by a
tc skbedit action, a netfilter rule or a privileged SO_PRIORITY, and is
kept. A priority set on the outer packet, in the OUTPUT or POSTROUTING
hook or by a tc filter on the egress device, still applies.

SAs that copy the DSCP keep the inner priority, and so do transport and
BEET mode SAs, which leave the DSCP of the packet on the wire. RFC 4301
section 4.1 expects a sender to put traffic classes that are scheduled
apart on separate SAs.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
--- a/net/xfrm/xfrm_output.c
+++ b/net/xfrm/xfrm_output.c
@@ -560,6 +560,22 @@ static void xfrm_set_flow_hash(struct sk
 	__skb_set_sw_hash(skb, flow_hash_from_keys(&keys), true);
 }
 
+/* A tunnel mode SA with XFRM_SA_XFLAG_DONT_ENCAP_DSCP keeps the class of
+ * the inner packet off the outer header. Drop the priority that came
+ * with the inner packet as well: qdiscs and drivers that schedule by
+ * skb->priority would otherwise split the SA by inner class and reorder
+ * its packets across the peer's replay window. A priority with a major
+ * number is a qdisc class id that was set explicitly, and is kept.
+ */
+static void xfrm_set_outer_priority(struct sk_buff *skb,
+				    const struct xfrm_state *x)
+{
+	if (x->props.mode == XFRM_MODE_TUNNEL &&
+	    (x->props.extra_flags & XFRM_SA_XFLAG_DONT_ENCAP_DSCP) &&
+	    !TC_H_MAJ(skb->priority))
+		skb->priority = 0;
+}
+
 static int xfrm_output_one(struct sk_buff *skb, int err)
 {
 	struct dst_entry *dst = skb_dst(skb);
@@ -593,6 +609,7 @@ static int xfrm_output_one(struct sk_buf
 		}
 
 		xfrm_set_flow_hash(skb, x);
+		xfrm_set_outer_priority(skb, x);
 
 		err = xfrm_dev_packet_state_check(x, skb);
 		if (err)
@@ -846,6 +863,7 @@ int xfrm_output(struct sock *sk, struct
 			goto state_invalid;
 		}
 
+		xfrm_set_outer_priority(skb, x);
 		if (ops->xdo_dev_packet_xmit) {
 			skb->mark = xfrm_smark_get(skb->mark, x);
 			err = ops->xdo_dev_packet_xmit(sk, skb, x);
